<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.14.6: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00624</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.14.6: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>fe383983-22b2-4e7d-bf71-1f773c05f698</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para dir="ltr"><emphasis><emphasis>Students will complete the following questions to practice the skills they have learned in this lesson.</emphasis></emphasis></para>
<para dir="ltr">In the following exercises, determine whether each ordered pair is a solution to the system.</para>
<para>1. \(3x + y &gt; 5\)
</para>
<para dir="ltr" role="presentation">     \(2x-y\leq10\)</para>
<para dir="ltr"> </para>
<para dir="ltr">a. \((3,−3)\) </para>
<para dir="ltr"><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>Yes
When you substitute the values into the inequalities the results are:</para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{rcr}
  3(3) + (-3) &gt; 5 &amp;   &amp;2(3) − (-3)&amp;leq;10 \\
  6&gt;5 \checkmark &amp;   &amp; 9&amp;leq;10 \checkmark
  \end{array}\) </para>
<para>The point is true for both inequalities, so the ordered pair (3, -3) is a solution to the system.</para>
<para dir="ltr">b.  \((7,1)\)</para><para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>No
When you substitute the values into the inequalities the results are:</para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{rcr}
  3(7) + (1) &gt; 5 &amp;   &amp;2(7) − (1)&amp;leq;10 \\
  22&gt;5 \checkmark &amp;   &amp; 13&amp;leq;10 ❌
  \end{array}\) </para>
<para>The point must be true for both inequalities to be a solution to the system. Since (7,1) is not true for both systems, it is not a solution.</para>

<para dir="ltr"> 2. \(4x − y &lt; 10\)    \(−2x + 2y &gt; −8\)</para>
<para dir="ltr"> </para>
<para dir="ltr">a.  \((5,−2)\) </para>
<para dir="ltr"><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>No 
When you substitute the values into the inequalities the results are:</para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{rcr}
  4(5) - (-2) &lt; 10 &amp;   &amp;-2(5) + 2(-2)&gt;−8 \\
  22&lt;10 ❌ &amp;   &amp; -14&gt;-8 ❌
  \end{array}\) </para>
  <para> Since (5,-2) is not true for either system, it is not a solution.</para>
<para dir="ltr">b. \((−1,3)\)</para><para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>Yes
When you substitute the values into the inequalities the results are:</para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{rcr}
  4(-1) - (3) &lt; 10 &amp;   &amp;-2(-1) + 2(3)&gt;−8 \\
  -7&lt;10 \checkmark &amp;   &amp; 8&gt;-8 \checkmark
  \end{array}\) </para>
  <para>The point is true for both inequalities, so the ordered pair (-1,3) is a solution to the system.</para>

<para>3.  \(y\;&gt;\;\frac23x\;-\;5\)      \(x\;+\;\frac12y\;\leq\;4\)</para>
<para dir="ltr"> </para>
<para dir="ltr">a.  \((6, −4)\) </para>
<para dir="ltr"><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>No
When you substitute the values into the inequalities the results are:</para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{rcr}
  -4 &gt; \frac23(6)-5 &amp;   &amp;6+\frac12(-4)&amp;leq;4 \\
  -4&gt;-1 ❌ &amp;   &amp; 4&amp;leq;4 \checkmark
  \end{array}\) </para>
  <para>The point must be true for both inequalities to be a solution to the system. Since (6,-4) is not true for both systems, it is not a solution.</para>
<para dir="ltr">b. \((3, 0)\)</para><para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>Yes
When you substitute the values into the inequalities the results are:</para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{rcr}
0 &gt; \frac23(3)-5 &amp;   &amp;3+\frac12(0)&amp;leq;4 \\
0&gt;-3 \checkmark &amp;   &amp; 3&amp;leq;4 \checkmark
\end{array}\) </para>
<para>The point is true for both inequalities, so the ordered pair (3,0) is a solution to the system.</para>

<para>4.  \(y\;&lt;\;\frac32x\;+\;3\)      \(\frac34x\;-\;2y\;&lt;\;5\)</para>
<para dir="ltr"> </para>
<para dir="ltr">a. \((−4,−1)\)</para>
<para dir="ltr"><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>No 
When you substitute the values into the inequalities the results are:</para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{rcr}
  -1 &lt; \frac32(-4)+3 &amp;   &amp;\frac34(-4)-2(-1)&lt;5 \\
  -1&lt;-3 ❌ &amp;   &amp; -1&lt;5 \checkmark
  \end{array}\) </para>
  <para>The point must be true for both inequalities to be a solution to the system. Since (-4,-1) is not true for both systems, it is not a solution.</para>

<para dir="ltr"> b. \((8, 3)\)</para><para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>Yes
  When you substitute the values into the inequalities the results are:</para>
  <para>\(\begin{array}{rcr}
    3 &lt; \frac32(8)+3 &amp;   &amp;\frac34(8)-2(3)&lt;5 \\
    3&lt;15 \checkmark &amp;   &amp; 0&lt;5 \checkmark
    \end{array}\) </para>
    <para>The point is true for both inequalities, so the ordered pair (8,3) is a solution to the system.</para>

<para>5.  \(6x − 5y &lt; 20\)      \(-2x+7y&gt;-8\)</para>
<para dir="ltr">a. \((1, −3)\) </para>
  <para dir="ltr"><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>No
When you substitute the values into the inequalities the results are:</para>
    <para>\(\begin{array}{rcr}
      6(1)-5(-3) &lt; 20 &amp;   &amp;-2(1)+7(-3)&gt;-8 \\
      21&lt;20 ❌ &amp;   &amp; -23&gt;-8 ❌
      \end{array}\) </para>
      <para> Since (1,-3) is not true for either system, it is not a solution.</para>
<para dir="ltr">b. \((−4, 4)\)</para><para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>Yes
  When you substitute the values into the inequalities the results are:</para>
  <para>\(\begin{array}{rcr}
    6(-4)-5(4) &lt; 20 &amp;   &amp;-2(-4)+7(4)&gt;-8 \\
    -44&lt;20 \checkmark &amp;   &amp; 36&gt;-8 \checkmark
    \end{array}\) </para>
    <para>The point is true for both inequalities, so the ordered pair (-4,4) is a solution to the system.</para>

<para dir="ltr"/>
<para>6. Two inequalities are graphed on the same coordinate plane. Which region
  represents the solution to the system of the two inequalities?</para>

<para dir="ltr"><figure><media alt="Two intersecting inequalities."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/7cb503fc3f5524052d06f4af2d1ad8835c95afcf"/></media></figure></para>
<para dir="ltr"><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>Region D </para>
<para>The correct answer is Region D. 
  Region D represents the overlap of the graph of the inequality represented by the area shaded blue and the graph of the inequality represented by the area that is striped. </para>
</section>
</content>
